---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArrowButton from '../components/ArrowButton.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const entries = await getCollection('etiquette');
const countryCount = new Set(entries.map((e) => e.data.country)).size;

const description =
  'Why Etiquetteness exists, how entries get sourced and checked, and what to do if one of them is wrong.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About Etiquetteness',
  description,
  url: new URL('/about', Astro.site),
};
---

<Layout title="About" description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="font-display text-5xl font-semibold">About Etiquetteness</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-stone-600">
      Most travel advice tells you what to see. Almost none of it tells you which hand to
      pour with, or why leaving your chopsticks standing in a bowl of rice can genuinely
      unsettle a room. Etiquetteness is a running collection of that second kind of
      knowledge: the unwritten rules people expect visitors to already know.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-stone-400">
      Currently {entries.length} rules across {countryCount} countries
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold">How an entry gets made</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600">
      Every rule here starts from a real source, cited and linked at the bottom of the
      entry, not a vague sense of "I heard somewhere that..." Each one also gets a plain
      explanation of the reasoning behind the custom, not just the rule itself, since
      "don't do X" is a lot easier to remember once you know why X reads as rude in the
      first place.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600">
      Sources get things wrong sometimes, and secondhand travel-blog etiquette advice has a
      way of copying the same error from site to site. Where possible, entries get checked
      against someone who actually grew up with the custom in question, rather than taken
      at face value from a single article.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold">If something here is wrong</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600">
      Etiquette is local, it changes over time, and a rule that's strict for one
      generation can be casual for the next. If an entry is outdated, overstated, or
      flatly wrong, that's worth fixing rather than leaving up. Treat anything here as a
      starting point, not the final word, especially against someone who actually lives
      the custom day to day.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600">
      The code and content are open source — open a pull request or an issue on <a href="https://github.com/wonsukchoi/etiquetteness" class="underline decoration-stone-300 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-stone-900">GitHub</a>, or just email <a href="mailto:wonsukchoi97@gmail.com" class="underline decoration-stone-300 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-stone-900">wonsukchoi97@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>

    <ArrowButton href="/" class="mt-10">Browse all entries</ArrowButton>
  </div>
</Layout>
